'''truculently''' (trö′kū-lent-li or truk′ū-lẹnt-li), ''adv.'' In a truculent manner; fiercely; destructively.

'''Trudeau’s tern.''' See ''tern''<sup>1</sup>.

'''trudge'''<sup>1</sup> (truj), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''trudged'', ppr. ''trudging''. [Formerly also ''tridge''; origin obscure. Connection with ''tread'', unless by confusion with ''drudge''<sup>1</sup>, is impossible. Skeat suggests as the prob. source Sw. dial. ''truga'' = Norw. ''truga'' = Icel. ''thrūga'', snow-shoe.] To make one’s way on foot; walk; travel on foot; especially, to travel wearily or laboriously on foot.
{{smaller block|Thence dyd I ''trudge'' hoamward, too learne yf she haplye returned. {{right|''Stanihurst'', Æneid, ii.}}
<poem>Nay, if you fall to fainting,
’Tis time for me to ''trudge''.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and Massinger''?), Lovers’ Progress, i. 2.}}
He was a faithful, affectionate, simple soul as ever ''trudged'' after the heels of a philosopher.
{{right|''Sterne'', Sentimental Journey, p. 33.}}}}

'''trudge'''<sup>1</sup> (truj), ''n.'' [< ''trudge''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] A weary or laborious walk or tramp. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|We set out for the two miles’ ''trudge'' to Doughtown.
{{right|''Arch. Forbes'', in Eng. Illust. Mag., Aug., 1884, p. 698.}}}}

'''trudge'''<sup>2</sup>† (truj), ''n.'' [Abbr. of ''trudgeman''.] An interpreter.
{{smaller block|One thing said twice (as we say commonly) deserueth a ''trudge''. {{right|''Lyly'', Euphues, Anat. of Wit, p. 137.}}}}

'''trudgeman'''†, ''n.'' See ''truchman''.

'''true''' (trö), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''trew'', ''trewe''; < ME. ''true'', ''truwe'', ''treue'', ''trewe'', ''triwe'', ''treowe'', < AS. ''treówe'', ''trȳwe'' (also ''getreówe'', ''getrȳwe'') = OS.

readings.c2s3:
triuwi = OFries. ''triuwe'' = D. ''trouw'' = MLG. ''truwe'', LG. ''trou'' = OHG. *''triuwi'', MHG. ''triuwe'', G. ''treu'' (also OHG. ''gitriuwi'', MHG. ''getriuwe'', G. ''getreu'') = Icel. ''tryggr'', ''trūr'' = Sw. ''trogen'' = Dan. ''tro'' = Goth. ''triggws'', true; from a root (Teut. √ ''tru'', Aryan √ ''dru'') seen also in ''trow''<sup>1</sup>, ''trust'', etc., and in OPruss. ''druwi'', ''druwis'', faith, ''druwit'', believe. Hence ult. ''true'', ''n.'', ''truce'', ''truth'', ''troth'', etc. Cf. also ''trow''<sup>1</sup>, ''trust''<sup>1</sup>, and ''trig''.] '''1.''' Conformable to fact; being in accordance with the actual state of things; not false, fictitious, or erroneous: as, a ''true'' story; a ''true'' statement.
{{smaller block|Sum Men seyn that thei ben Sepultures of grete Lordes, that weren somtyme; but that is not ''trewe''.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 52.}}}}
{{smaller block|What proposition is there respecting human nature which is absolutely and universally ''true''?
{{right|''Macaulay'', Mill on Government.}}}}
{{smaller block|[''True'' in this sense is often used elliptically for ''that is true'', or ''it is true''.

''True'', I have married her. {{right|''Shak.'', Othello, i. 3. 79.}}

''Cham.'' Your only road now, sir, is York, York, sir.
''Green.'' ''True'', but yet it comes scant of the prophecy: Lincoln was, London is, and York shall be.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 1.]}}}}

'''2.''' Conformable to reason or to established rules or custom; exact; just; accurate; correct.
{{smaller block|They were all illiterate men; the ablest of them could not write ''true'' English — no, not common words.
{{right|''Winthrop'', Hist. New England, II. 175.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Apelles drew
A Circle regularly ''true''.</poem>
{{right|''Prior'', Protogenes and Apelles.}}}}
{{smaller block|A translation nicely ''true'' to the original. {{right|''Arbuthnot.''}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|It is not always that its [the trumpet’s] notes are either ''true'' or tuneful. {{right|''Dickens'', Martin Chuzzlewit, xii.}}}}
'''3.''' Conformable to law and justice; legitimate; rightful: as, the ''true'' heir.
{{smaller block|<poem>An oath is of no moment, being not took
Before a ''true'' and lawful magistrate.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., i. 2. 23.}}}}
'''4.''' Conformable to nature; natural; correct.
{{smaller block|No shape so ''true'', no truth of such account.{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, lxii.}}}}
'''5.''' In ''biol.'': (''a'') Conforming or conformable to a type, norm, or standard of structure; typical: as, an amœba is a ''true'' animal; a canary is a ''true'' bird; the lion is a ''true'' cat; a frog or toad is not a ''true'' reptile. (''b'') Genuine; truebred; not hybrid or mongrel: as, a ''true'' merino sheep. Also used adverbially: as, to breed ''true''.—'''6.''' Genuine; pure; real; not counterfeit, adulterated, false, or pretended.
{{smaller block|For vntrue praise neuer giueth any ''true'' reputation.{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 22.}}
Never call a ''true'' piece of gold a counterfeit.{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., ii. 4. 539.}}
<poem>Unbind the charms that in slight fables lie,
And teach that truth is ''truest'' poetry.</poem>{{right|''Cowley.''}}}}
'''7.''' In ''anat.'', complete; perfected: as, ''true'' ribs (that is, those which articulate with the breast-

readings.c3s2:
bone, as distinguished from false or floating ribs); the ''true'' pelvis (that part of the pelvis below the superior strait or iliopectineal line); a ''true'' corpus luteum (the complete corpus luteum of pregnancy, as distinguished from the same body unaffected by the result of conception).—'''8.''' Free from falsehood; habitually speaking the truth; veracious; truthful.
{{smaller block|Master, we know that thou art ''true'', and teachest the way of God in truth.
{{right|Mat. xxii. 16.}}
<poem>I am too plain and ''true'' to be suspected.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Valentinian, iv. 2.}}}}
'''9.''' Firm or steady in adhering to promises, to friends, to one’s principles, etc.; not fickle, false, or perfidious; faithful; constant; loyal.
{{smaller block|Ne noon may be ''trewe'' to hym-self but he first be ''trewe'' to God.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), i. 55.}}
<poem>Fair is my love, but not so fair as fickle;
Mild as a dove, but neither ''true'' nor trusty.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Passionate Pilgrim, l. 86.}}
There is no such Treasure as a ''true'' Friend.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. vi. 56.}}
<poem>A mercenary Jilt, and ''true'' to no Man.</poem>
{{right|''Wycherley'', Plain Dealer, Prol.}}
He had seen the path of duty plain before him. Through good and evil he was to be ''true'' to Church and king.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., vi.}}}}
'''10.''' Honest.
{{smaller block|<poem>For why a ''trewe'' man, withouten drede,
Hath nat to parten with a theves dede.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 464.}}
Rich preys make ''true'' men thieves.
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 724.}}}}
'''11.''' Sure; unerring; unfailing.

